This is the fix for the issue. The bug is that when `cbytes < 0`, the code reads `src[0]` as the token without checking if there's enough space. If `srcsize` is 0 (or less than 1 byte), then `src[0]` reads uninitialized memory.

Wait, but let me re-examine. The issue is a use-of-uninitialized-value. Let me look at the flow more carefully.

In the loop:
```c
for (int j = 0; j < nstreams; j++) {
    if (srcsize < sizeof(int32_t)) {
      return -1;
    }
    srcsize -= sizeof(int32_t);
    cbytes = sw32_(src);      /* amount of compressed bytes */
    if (cbytes > 0) {
      if (srcsize < cbytes) {
        return -1;
      }
      srcsize -= cbytes;
    }
    src += sizeof(int32_t);
    ctbytes += (int32_t)sizeof(int32_t);

    /* Uncompress */
    if (cbytes == 0) {
      ...
    }
    else if (cbytes < 0) {
      // A negative number means some encoding depending on the token that comes next
      uint8_t token = src[0];   // <-- reads src[0] without checking srcsize
      src += 1;
      ctbytes += 1;
      if (token & 0x1) {
        ...
      }
    }
    ...
}
```

So when `cbytes < 0`, the code reads `src[0]` as the token. But it doesn't check if `srcsize >= 1`. If `srcsize == 0` at this point, then `src[0]` reads beyond the valid input buffer, which is uninitialized memory (MSAN).

